# Playwright QA — Pipit House

Use these rules when writing or editing end-to-end tests. Stack: Playwright + TypeScript (strict) against this Next.js App Router site (`npm run dev`, base URL `http://localhost:3000`).

## Locators (accessibility first)

Prefer user-facing locators in this order: `getByRole` → `getByLabel` → `getByPlaceholder` → `getByAltText` → `getByText`. Use `getByTestId` only when the UI has no accessible name. Do not use CSS selectors, XPath, or chained `.locator('div.foo')` unless no accessible locator exists.

Scope to landmark roles already in the app (`navigation` named Primary / Footer) so duplicate names do not collide.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — brittle, not how a guest finds the page
await page.locator("nav a:nth-child(3)").click();
await page.waitForSelector(".text-harbour-900");

// ✅ GOOD — role + accessible name
const primaryNav = page.getByRole("navigation", { name: "Primary" });
await primaryNav.getByRole("link", { name: "The Cottage" }).click();
await expect(page.getByRole("heading", { level: 1 })).toBeVisible();
```

Public routes: `/`, `/the-cottage`, `/gallery`, `/blog`, `/location`, `/contact`, `/availability`. “Check availability” is an external Sykes link (`target="_blank"`); assert `href` / popup, do not complete a real booking.

## Page Object Model

Put locators and guest actions in page objects. Specs describe behaviour only. One page object per route (or shared chrome: header/footer). Constructor takes `Page`. Expose methods (`goto`, `openCottage`, `expectHeading`), not raw locators, unless a spec needs a one-off assertion.

```typescript
// e2e/pages/home.page.ts
export class HomePage {
  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {}

  readonly primaryNav = this.page.getByRole("navigation", { name: "Primary" });

  async goto(): Promise<void> {
    await this.page.goto("/");
  }

  async openGallery(): Promise<void> {
    await this.primaryNav.getByRole("link", { name: "Gallery" }).click();
    await expect(this.page).toHaveURL(/\/gallery$/);
  }
}
```

Do not share mutable state between tests. Create a fresh page object in `test.beforeEach` or a fixture.

## Auto-waiting

Trust Playwright’s web-first assertions and actionability checks. Never use `page.waitForTimeout()`. Prefer `expect(locator).toBeVisible()`, `toHaveURL()`, `toHaveAttribute()`, `toHaveCount()`. Use `expect.poll` or `toPass` only for conditions Playwright cannot auto-wait. Hard-wait only if a third-party script has no observable UI; document why.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
await page.click("text=Gallery");
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);

// ✅ GOOD
await homePage.openGallery();
await expect(page.getByRole("heading", { name: "Gallery" })).toBeVisible();
```

Keep tests isolated and deterministic. Use `test.use` / project fixtures for viewport. Do not rely on production data that can go missing (events on `/blog` are generated).

## TypeScript strict mode

Match repo `tsconfig` (`strict: true`). No `any`, no unchecked `as`. Type `Page`, `Locator`, `BrowserContext`. Use `import type` for type-only imports. Page object methods return `Promise<void>` (or a typed result). Prefer `satisfies` for config objects.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
async clickThing(el: any) {
  await el.click();
}

// ✅ GOOD
async clickCta(name: string): Promise<void> {
  await this.page.getByRole("link", { name }).click();
}
```

Playwright config must use `defineConfig` with TypeScript. Point `webServer` at `npm run dev` (or `npm run start` after build) and `baseURL` at the local origin. Colocate tests under `e2e/` (specs `*.spec.ts`, page objects `e2e/pages/`).
